1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:02,561
July sixth, nineteen sixty-seven.

2
00:00:02,839 --> 00:00:07,168
The research submarine Alvin is two
thousand feet down, off the coast of

3
00:00:07,168 --> 00:00:07,649
Florida.

4
00:00:08,144 --> 00:00:09,926
Down here, there is no light.

5
00:00:10,254 --> 00:00:11,009
No sound.

6
00:00:11,326 --> 00:00:13,651
Nothing should be moving fast enough to
hit you.

7
00:00:14,152 --> 00:00:15,214
Then something does.

8
00:00:15,734 --> 00:00:19,606
When Alvin finally reaches the surface,
the crew finds the problem.

9
00:00:20,190 --> 00:00:22,279
Stuck in the hull is a swordfish.

10
00:00:22,462 --> 00:00:27,611
Its sword driven into a gap in the sub's
outer skin, the rest of it still attached.

11
00:00:27,841 --> 00:00:29,860
A fish attacked a submarine.

12
00:00:30,035 --> 00:00:32,049
And the submarine had to retreat.

13
00:00:32,258 --> 00:00:37,622
This is a file about the only fish in the
ocean that are born with a weapon on their

14
00:00:37,622 --> 00:00:37,941
face.

15
00:00:37,941 --> 00:00:40,618
Swordfish, marlin and their relatives.

16
00:00:40,897 --> 00:00:44,550
Animals that have pierced boats, ships,
submarines, and sometimes, people.

17
00:00:44,977 --> 00:00:45,908
But here's the strangest part.

18
00:00:46,368 --> 00:00:46,834
That sword?

19
00:00:47,297 --> 00:00:48,709
It isn't really built for stabbing.

20
00:00:49,104 --> 00:00:49,780
Remember that.

21
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:51,836
We'll come back to it.

22
00:00:52,126 --> 00:00:52,878
Encounter File number two.

23
00:00:53,242 --> 00:00:54,218
The billfish.

24
00:00:54,734 --> 00:00:57,519
There are a few members of this family.

25
00:00:57,930 --> 00:00:59,631
Marlin, with a round, spear-like bill.

26
00:01:00,055 --> 00:01:02,536
Sailfish, with a huge fin on their back.

27
00:01:02,910 --> 00:01:08,325
And the swordfish, with a flat, sharp-
edged sword that can be a third of its

28
00:01:08,325 --> 00:01:09,169
body length.

29
00:01:09,169 --> 00:01:10,758
They're not small.

30
00:01:10,758 --> 00:01:16,763
The heaviest swordfish ever caught on a
rod, off Chile in nineteen fifty-three,

31
00:01:16,763 --> 00:01:19,499
weighed over five hundred kilograms.

32
00:01:19,499 --> 00:01:21,754
Big marlin can top half a ton.

33
00:01:22,011 --> 00:01:24,236
And the swordfish has one more trick.

34
00:01:24,819 --> 00:01:29,730
During the day it hunts hundreds of metres
down, where the water is cold and almost

35
00:01:29,730 --> 00:01:30,085
black.

36
00:01:30,544 --> 00:01:32,284
Cold eyes are slow eyes.

37
00:01:32,722 --> 00:01:34,580
So the swordfish carries a heater.

38
00:01:35,035 --> 00:01:39,833
A special organ, made from a modified eye
muscle, that warms its eyes and brain well

39
00:01:39,833 --> 00:01:42,404
above the temperature of the water around
it.

40
00:01:42,791 --> 00:01:47,945
Scientists found that warm eyes can track
fast movement many times better than cold

41
00:01:47,945 --> 00:01:48,255
ones.

42
00:01:48,255 --> 00:01:52,390
In the dark, the swordfish sees you move
before you know it's there.

43
00:01:52,390 --> 00:01:57,032
If you've read anything about these fish,
you've probably seen this number.

44
00:01:57,032 --> 00:01:59,468
The sailfish, fastest fish in the ocean.

45
00:01:59,818 --> 00:02:01,961
One hundred and ten kilometres an hour.

46
00:02:02,085 --> 00:02:03,348
It's a great number.

47
00:02:03,541 --> 00:02:05,899
It's also, almost certainly, wrong.

48
00:02:06,039 --> 00:02:10,681
Those old speeds came from fishing reels,
from timing how fast line was ripped off

49
00:02:10,681 --> 00:02:11,247
the spool.

50
00:02:11,710 --> 00:02:13,854
That measures the line, not the fish.

51
00:02:14,073 --> 00:02:18,262
So in twenty sixteen, a team of scientists
tried something smarter.

52
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:23,618
They measured how fast the swimming
muscles can actually contract.

53
00:02:23,739 --> 00:02:27,007
A fish can only beat its tail as fast as
its muscles allow.

54
00:02:27,007 --> 00:02:27,851
Their answer?

55
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:31,524
A sailfish tops out at roughly eight
metres per second.

56
00:02:31,791 --> 00:02:33,610
About thirty kilometres an hour.

57
00:02:33,962 --> 00:02:35,821
And there's a hard limit above that.

58
00:02:36,105 --> 00:02:41,196
Push past ten to fifteen metres a second,
and the water pressure around the fins

59
00:02:41,196 --> 00:02:44,442
drops so fast it literally boils into tiny
bubbles.

60
00:02:44,442 --> 00:02:48,794
Those bubbles collapse against the fin and
tear the tissue apart.

61
00:02:48,952 --> 00:02:51,822
The ocean itself puts a speed limit on
fish.

62
00:02:51,976 --> 00:02:54,904
Thirty kilometres an hour doesn't sound
like much.

63
00:02:55,191 --> 00:02:58,576
Until you remember what's attached to the
front.

64
00:02:58,929 --> 00:03:00,692
So, what is the sword actually for?

65
00:03:01,270 --> 00:03:06,439
In twenty fourteen, researchers filmed
sailfish hunting sardines off Cancún, with

66
00:03:06,439 --> 00:03:07,652
high-speed cameras.

67
00:03:08,092 --> 00:03:09,580
What they saw surprised them.

68
00:03:09,955 --> 00:03:11,987
The sailfish didn't charge in and stab.

69
00:03:12,503 --> 00:03:17,634
They slid their bills into the school,
slowly, so quietly the sardines didn't

70
00:03:17,634 --> 00:03:18,367
even react.

71
00:03:18,367 --> 00:03:20,158
Then, a flick of the head.

72
00:03:20,330 --> 00:03:22,547
A sideways slash, or a quick tap.

73
00:03:22,547 --> 00:03:26,049
Only about one slash in ten caught a fish
outright.

74
00:03:26,191 --> 00:03:31,495
The rest just injured them, knocking off
scales, cutting tissue, until the school

75
00:03:31,495 --> 00:03:32,936
started to fall apart.

76
00:03:33,201 --> 00:03:37,339
So the bill works less like a spear, and
more like a sword being swung.

77
00:03:37,537 --> 00:03:40,641
Scientists think swordfish use theirs in a
similar way.

78
00:03:40,852 --> 00:03:44,880
Which means that when a billfish puts its
bill through something, it usually isn't

79
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:45,273
hunting.

80
00:03:45,555 --> 00:03:49,170
And as you're about to see, it's almost
always the same story.

81
00:03:49,653 --> 00:03:52,957
A fish that's hooked, speared, or flying
through the air.

82
00:03:53,607 --> 00:03:54,873
Let's start with ships.

83
00:03:55,342 --> 00:04:00,840
In eighteen thirty-two, a sailing ship
called the Farquharson came into port with

84
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:02,741
something stuck in its hull.

85
00:04:03,074 --> 00:04:05,821
Two bills, from two marlin, driven deep
into the timber.

86
00:04:06,276 --> 00:04:09,595
That plank is still kept at the Natural
History Museum in London.

87
00:04:10,057 --> 00:04:11,013
The fish lost.

88
00:04:11,292 --> 00:04:12,852
The wood still has the holes.

89
00:04:13,218 --> 00:04:14,125
Now, people.

90
00:04:14,568 --> 00:04:18,406
In twenty twelve, off northern Australia,
a charter crew was reversing their boat

91
00:04:18,406 --> 00:04:22,340
toward a hooked black marlin of around two
hundred and seventy kilograms, to tag it

92
00:04:22,340 --> 00:04:23,003
and let it go.

93
00:04:23,357 --> 00:04:24,051
The marlin jumped.

94
00:04:24,193 --> 00:04:24,974
Not away from the boat.

95
00:04:25,366 --> 00:04:25,725
Into it.

96
00:04:25,725 --> 00:04:29,506
It crashed through the cockpit, knocked
people over, sent a chair flying, and then

97
00:04:29,506 --> 00:04:30,936
threw itself back into the sea.

98
00:04:31,056 --> 00:04:33,434
Somehow, nobody was seriously hurt.

99
00:04:33,434 --> 00:04:35,355
It was all caught on camera.

100
00:04:35,355 --> 00:04:40,509
In twenty fifteen, off Hawaii, fisherman
Russell Ching spent a long fight bringing

101
00:04:40,509 --> 00:04:43,778
in a blue marlin of more than two hundred
kilograms.

102
00:04:43,778 --> 00:04:48,450
As the crew hauled it aboard, the weight
shifted, and the bill went straight

103
00:04:48,450 --> 00:04:49,433
through his leg.

104
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:52,071
It missed the main artery.

105
00:04:52,071 --> 00:04:55,917
He walked away, and kept the tip of the
bill.

106
00:04:55,917 --> 00:04:58,852
Not every story ends like that.

107
00:04:58,852 --> 00:05:05,691
Earlier that same year, in a harbour on
Hawaii's Big Island, charter captain Randy

108
00:05:05,691 --> 00:05:10,862
Llanes went into the water with a spear
gun after a swordfish.

109
00:05:11,129 --> 00:05:11,814
He hit it.

110
00:05:12,136 --> 00:05:15,636
The wounded fish thrashed, and its bill
struck him in the chest.

111
00:05:16,088 --> 00:05:17,177
He didn't survive.

112
00:05:17,596 --> 00:05:19,562
Investigators ruled it an accident.

113
00:05:20,059 --> 00:05:25,285
And in twenty twenty-four, in Indonesia's
Mentawai Islands, Italian surfer Giulia

114
00:05:25,285 --> 00:05:29,866
Manfrini was struck in the chest by a fish
that leapt out of the water.

115
00:05:30,317 --> 00:05:31,715
Officials reported it as a swordfish.

116
00:05:31,810 --> 00:05:36,177
The resort where she was staying said it
was a different fish entirely, a

117
00:05:36,177 --> 00:05:36,835
needlefish.

118
00:05:36,835 --> 00:05:38,433
We may never know which.

119
00:05:38,433 --> 00:05:41,437
We'll come back to that other fish in a
future file.

120
00:05:41,543 --> 00:05:42,894
Look at the pattern.

121
00:05:42,894 --> 00:05:43,947
A hooked fish.

122
00:05:43,947 --> 00:05:45,070
A speared fish.

123
00:05:45,070 --> 00:05:46,130
A fish in mid-air.

124
00:05:46,397 --> 00:05:48,787
None of these animals came looking for a
person.

125
00:05:49,281 --> 00:05:50,570
So let's put you on the boat.

126
00:05:50,775 --> 00:05:51,510
Small boat.

127
00:05:51,776 --> 00:05:52,496
Open ocean.

128
00:05:52,859 --> 00:05:57,090
The line is screaming off the reel, and
three hundred kilos of marlin just came

129
00:05:57,090 --> 00:05:59,072
out of the water, twenty metres away.

130
00:05:59,500 --> 00:06:00,257
Heading your way.

131
00:06:00,607 --> 00:06:01,189
Your move.

132
00:06:01,757 --> 00:06:02,507
Option one.

133
00:06:02,956 --> 00:06:04,626
Grab the line and pull it in.

134
00:06:05,076 --> 00:06:05,782
Bad idea.

135
00:06:06,214 --> 00:06:10,396
That line is attached to an animal that
can outpull you without trying, and

136
00:06:10,396 --> 00:06:13,854
pulling it closer is exactly how the bill
ends up in the boat.

137
00:06:14,264 --> 00:06:15,033
Option two.

138
00:06:15,523 --> 00:06:17,570
Lean over the side to get a better look.

139
00:06:18,032 --> 00:06:19,699
This is the one that hurts people.

140
00:06:20,357 --> 00:06:22,706
The edge of the boat is where the fish
arrives.

141
00:06:23,175 --> 00:06:24,014
Option three.

142
00:06:24,323 --> 00:06:26,261
Jump in the water and swim for it.

143
00:06:26,694 --> 00:06:30,247
Now you're in its world, next to a
frightened animal with a sword.

144
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:33,054
That's the situation that killed a captain
in Hawaii.

145
00:06:33,512 --> 00:06:34,319
Option four.

146
00:06:34,713 --> 00:06:37,530
Get low, and get away from the side the
fish is on.

147
00:06:37,773 --> 00:06:38,451
That's the one.

148
00:06:38,795 --> 00:06:42,451
Crews who catch these fish for a living
have a few simple rules.

149
00:06:42,696 --> 00:06:45,125
Keep everyone clear of the side where the
fish is.

150
00:06:45,575 --> 00:06:48,026
Never put your body between the bill and
the boat.

151
00:06:48,458 --> 00:06:50,388
Control the bill before anything else.

152
00:06:50,878 --> 00:06:53,586
And more and more, they don't bring the
fish aboard at all.

153
00:06:54,093 --> 00:06:57,312
They tag it in the water, cut it loose,
and let it go.

154
00:06:58,051 --> 00:07:03,025
Turns out the safest way to handle a sword
is to leave it in the ocean.

155
00:07:03,402 --> 00:07:04,096
And now, the twist.

156
00:07:04,399 --> 00:07:06,529
Every story in this file is real.

157
00:07:06,981 --> 00:07:11,589
But spread them across the decades, and
they add up to a handful of accidents.

158
00:07:11,841 --> 00:07:14,711
Rare enough that each one made the news
around the world.

159
00:07:15,561 --> 00:07:18,238
The other side of the ledger looks very
different.

160
00:07:18,778 --> 00:07:23,590
By the nineteen nineties, the North
Atlantic swordfish was in serious trouble.

161
00:07:24,082 --> 00:07:28,472
Fishing fleets were catching them faster
than they could breed, and more and more

162
00:07:28,472 --> 00:07:32,428
of the fish being landed were young ones
that had never had the chance to

163
00:07:32,428 --> 00:07:32,970
reproduce.

164
00:07:33,500 --> 00:07:35,346
So something unusual happened.

165
00:07:35,614 --> 00:07:37,139
Countries actually agreed.

166
00:07:37,366 --> 00:07:40,332
Minimum sizes, so young fish were thrown
back.

167
00:07:40,614 --> 00:07:43,499
Catch limits, and a rebuilding plan with a
deadline.

168
00:07:43,741 --> 00:07:44,566
And it worked.

169
00:07:44,812 --> 00:07:48,381
In two thousand and nine, the
international commission that manages

170
00:07:48,381 --> 00:07:51,736
these fish declared the North Atlantic
swordfish stock rebuilt.

171
00:07:52,303 --> 00:07:53,743
A year ahead of schedule.

172
00:07:53,743 --> 00:07:58,697
In an ocean full of collapse stories, the
swordfish is one of the rare comebacks.

173
00:07:58,697 --> 00:08:01,426
Not because it fought back with its sword.

174
00:08:01,426 --> 00:08:03,600
Because, for once, we put ours down.

175
00:08:03,704 --> 00:08:05,411
Back to nineteen sixty-seven.

176
00:08:05,512 --> 00:08:09,441
Alvin is back on deck, with a swordfish
still stuck in its side.

177
00:08:09,914 --> 00:08:12,698
It took the crew about two hours to work
the sword free.

178
00:08:13,178 --> 00:08:16,085
And then they did what sailors have always
done with a big fish.

179
00:08:16,499 --> 00:08:17,246
They cooked it.

180
00:08:17,748 --> 00:08:20,907
More than fifty kilograms of swordfish,
served for dinner.

181
00:08:21,211 --> 00:08:24,718
So in the only recorded fight between a
swordfish and a submarine, the submarine

182
00:08:24,718 --> 00:08:24,893
won.

183
00:08:25,298 --> 00:08:26,443
And then it ate the evidence.

184
00:08:26,993 --> 00:08:28,453
That was Encounter File number two.

185
00:08:29,167 --> 00:08:33,802
And if you're wondering about the other
fish, the one that may have struck that

186
00:08:33,802 --> 00:08:38,672
surfer in Indonesia, the one that flies at
fishermen's lights at night... that file

187
00:08:38,672 --> 00:08:39,611
is already open.

188
00:08:39,611 --> 00:08:42,814
Tell me in the comments what you want
after that.

189
00:08:42,814 --> 00:08:44,527
I read every single one.

